# Line endings are LF everywhere, working tree included: development happens on
# Windows and CI on Linux, and without this the tree is platform-dependent.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The distribution tarball carries consumption, not development. This file is
# per-variant because what counts as "development" is: a package that ships
# configuration distributes the very .dist files a library hides.
/.github/                export-ignore
/.githooks/              export-ignore
/.rak200                 export-ignore
/.gitmodules             export-ignore
/tests/                  export-ignore
/docs/                   export-ignore
/.editorconfig           export-ignore
/.gitattributes          export-ignore
/.gitignore              export-ignore
/.git-blame-ignore-revs  export-ignore
/.coverage-floor         export-ignore
/CLAUDE.md               export-ignore
/ROADMAP.md              export-ignore
/ARCHITECTURE.md         export-ignore

# A configuration package still has development of its own, and this is where the
# two lists part company. The consumer-side configs here — the ones without `.base`
# in the name — configure *this* package's own run: they name its src/ and its
# tests/, and a consumer that received one would be configuring nothing. Release
# plumbing is the same kind of thing.
#
# These lines arrived when the package acquired a suite. Before that it had no
# development to separate, which is why a config variant looked for a while like a
# variant that exports everything. Note what is NOT here: `*.base.*` is the product,
# and shipping it is the entire point of the package.
/eslint.config.js               export-ignore
/prettier.config.js             export-ignore
/stryker.config.js              export-ignore
/vitest.config.js               export-ignore
/tsconfig.json                  export-ignore
/.prettierignore                export-ignore
/release-please-config.json     export-ignore
/.release-please-manifest.json  export-ignore

# Deliberately NOT export-ignored here, and this is the whole reason the file is
# per-variant: in a configuration package the shared configs and CONVENTIONS.md are
# the product. The `ts` variant hides exactly what this one must ship. `src/` and
# `bin/` join them: a config package that ships an executable ships the code behind
# it.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOT GOVERN, so nobody edits it expecting the wrong effect:
# `export-ignore` applies to `git archive` — GitHub's source tarballs and zips, and
# a Composer dist fetched from them. **npm never reads it.** What a package published
# to npm ships is the `files` array in package.json, and what a package installed as
# a git dependency ships is the same `files` array after npm clones and packs. So on
# the TypeScript side the two lists live in different files, and only one of them
# reaches a consumer through the registry. Keep them saying the same thing anyway:
# the tarball a human downloads from GitHub should not disagree with the one npm
# installs about what this package is.
